syntax:
For a dependent template name in C++:
- Incorrect (ambiguous): dependent_expression->member_name<args>;
  // Here, '<' may be parsed as less-than operator.
- Correct (disambiguated): dependent_expression->template member_name<args>;
  // 'template' keyword ensures '<' starts a template argument list.
Implementation notes: The 'template' keyword is only needed in dependent contexts (e.g., involving template parameters). The compiler, during parsing, uses this to adjust the precedence and grouping in the AST, preventing misinterpretation as a relational operator.

code_example:
#include <iostream>
template<class T>
struct SomeClass {
    template<int N>
    T* alloc() { return new T[N]; }  // Example template member
    template<int M>
    static void adjust() { /* Implementation */ }
};

template<class T>
void f(T* p) {
    T* p1 = p->alloc<200>();  // Ill-formed: '<' interpreted as less than
    T* p2 = p->template alloc<200>();  // OK: 'template' disambiguates
    T::adjust<100>();  // Ill-formed: '<' interpreted as less than
    T::template adjust<100>();  // OK: 'template' disambiguates
}

int main() {
    SomeClass<int> obj;
    f(&obj);  // Calls the function with disambiguation
    return 0;
}
